    sku = item.get(\"sku\")\n            sku_units[sku] = sku_units.get(sku, 0) + qty\n        if not valid and order.get(\"strict\"):\n            problems.append(\"order %s: skipped (strict)\" % order.get(\"id\"))\n            continue\n        coupon = order.get(\"coupon\")\n        discount = 0.0\n        if coupon == \"TEN\" and subtotal >= 100:\n            discount = subtotal * 0.10\n        elif coupon == \"FIVE\":\n            discount = min(5.0, subtotal)\n        elif coupon == \"HALF\" and order.get(\"customer\") == \"staff\":\n            discount = subtotal * 0.5\n        taxable = subtotal - discount\n        country = order.get(\"country\", \"US\")\n        tax = round(taxable * TAX_RATES.get(country, 0.0), 2)\n        if taxable >= 50:\n            shipping = 0.0\n        elif country != \"US\":\n            shipping = 9.99\n        else:\n            shipping = 4.99\n        total = round(taxable + tax + shipping, 2)\n        customer = order.get(\"customer\", \"unknown\")\n        entry = totals.setdefault(customer, {\"orders\": 0, \"spent\": 0.0})\n        entry[\"orders\"] += 1\n        entry[\"spent\"] = round(entry[\"spent\"] + total, 2)\n        revenue += total\n    top_sku = None\n    if sku_units:\n        top_sku = sorted(sku_units.items(), key=lambda pair: (-pair[1], str(pair[0])))[0][0]\n    best = None\n    for customer, entry in totals.items():\n        if best is None or entry[\"spent\"] > totals[best][\"spent\"]:\n            best = customer\n    return {\n        \"revenue\": round(revenue, 2),\n        \"customers\": totals,\n        \"status_counts\": status_counts,\n        \"top_sku\": top_sku,\n        \"best_customer\": best,\n        \"problems\": problems,\n    }\n```\n\nImportant subtleties:\n1. `sku_units` accumulates for items of cancelled orders? No — cancelled orders `continue` before the item loop, so cancelled items are skipped. Good.\n2. For strict orders with bad items: the sku_units for the *valid* items of that strict order ARE added (since the item loop continues adding sku_units for valid items), but the order is skipped afterwards. So sku_units include valid items from skipped strict orders. Important to preserve!\n3. `revenue += total` accumulates unrounded-sum of rounded totals; final round(revenue, 2).\n4. `entry[\"spent\"] = round(entry[\"spent\"] + total, 2)` — per-customer rounding at each step.\n5. `top_sku`: sorted by (-units, str(sku)); picks the first. Note: sorting is stable, so ties broken by str(sku) ascending.\n6. `best_customer`: first customer (in insertion order of totals dict) with strictly greater spent. Ties keep the earlier one.\n7. Note: `status_counts` counts all orders including cancelled.\n8. Note the `tax` rounding: `round(taxable * TAX_RATES.get(country, 0.0), 2)` — uses banker's rounding on floats.\n9. `total = round(taxable + tax + shipping, 2)` — note taxable is unrounded, tax is rounded, shipping rounded.\n10. The order of `problems`: bad item problems appended during item iteration, then the \"skipped (strict)\" problem appended after the loop. Since all item problems for one order precede the strict message, order is preserved naturally.\n\nAlso key order in the returned dict: revenue, customers, status_counts, top_sku, best_customer, problems. And per-customer entry dict key order: {\"orders\": 0, \"spent\": 0.0} → orders first, spent second.\n\nNow let me design the refactor into small functions, each ≤ 30 lines.\n\nProposed structure:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Order summaries for the monthly report.\"\"\"\n\nTAX_RATES = {\"US\": 0.07, \"DE\": 0.19, \"FR\": 0.2}\n\nFREE_SHIPPING_THRESHOLD = 50.0\nDOMESTIC_SHIPPING = 4.99\nINTERNATIONAL_SHIPPING = 9.99\n\n\ndef _count_status(order, status_counts):\n    ...\n\ndef _collect_items(order, sku_units, problems):\n    \"\"\"Returns (subtotal, valid)\"\"\"\n```\n\nWait — one important detail: `_collect_items` must record sku_units even for strict-skipped orders, and problems. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef _scan_items(order, sku_units, problems):\n    subtotal = 0.0\n    valid = True\n    for item in order.get(\"items\", []):\n        qty = item.get(\"qty\", 0)\n        price = item.get(\"price\", 0.0)\n        if qty <= 0 or price < 0:\n            problems.append(\"order %s: bad item %s\" % (order.get(\"id\"), item.get(\"sku\")))\n            valid = False\n            continue\n        subtotal += qty * price\n        sku = item.get(\"sku\")\n        sku_units[sku] = sku_units.get(sku, 0) + qty\n    return subtotal, valid\n```\n\nThat's 15 lines. Good.\n\n```python\ndef _discount_for(order, subtotal):\n    coupon = order.get(\"coupon\")\n    if coupon == \"TEN\" and subtotal >= 100:\n        return subtotal * 0.10\n    if coupon == \"FIVE\":\n        return min(5.0, subtotal)\n    if coupon == \"HALF\" and order.get(\"customer\") == \"staff\":\n        return subtotal * 0.5\n    return 0.0\n```\n\nNote: original uses if/elif/elif chain; my version with separate ifs is equivalent since conditions are mutually exclusive on the coupon value (each branch checks a distinct coupon string). Careful: `coupon == \"TEN\"` and `coupon == \"FIVE\"` are mutually exclusive. `coupon == \"HALF\"` also exclusive. Yes equivalent.\n\n```python\ndef _tax_for(taxable, country):\n    return round(taxable * TAX_RATES.get(country, 0.0), 2)\n\n\ndef _shipping_for(taxable, country):\n    if taxable >= 50:\n        return 0.0\n    if country != \"US\":\n        return 9.99\n    return 4.99\n```\n\nHmm, `taxable >= 50` — original literal 50, and 50.0 == 50 so equivalent.\n\n```python\ndef _order_total(order, subtotal):\n    coupon discount...\n```\n\nLet me now write the per-order processing:\n\n```python\ndef _process_order(order, state):\n    ...\n```\n\nMaybe better to use a small accumulator class or dict. But keeping it simple: a module-level helper that takes explicit params and returns values. Let's design a helper that processes a single order and returns (total, customer) or None if skipped.\n\nApproach: create a small internal class `Summary`? The prompt says \"small, well-named functions\". A class is fine but let's keep functions.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\ndef summarize(orders):\n    \"\"\"Summarize a list of order dicts into revenue, per-customer totals and counts.\"\"\"\n    state = _State()\n    for order in orders:\n        _process_order(order, state)\n    return _build_report(state)\n```\n\nHmm, using a state object with mutable collections is a clean approach. But maybe simpler and more readable: pass explicit dicts.\n\nLet me write it as:\n\n```python\ndef _process_order(order, totals, status_counts, sku_units, problems):\n    \"\"\"Fold one order into the accumulators; returns the order total or None if skipped.\"\"\"\n    status = order.get(\"status\", \"new\")\n    status_counts[status] = status_counts.get(status, 0) + 1\n    if status == \"cancelled\":\n        return None\n    subtotal, valid = _scan_items(order, sku_units, problems)\n    if not valid and order.get(\"strict\"):\n        problems.append(\"order %s: skipped (strict)\" % order.get(\"id\"))\n        return None\n    taxable = subtotal - _discount_for(order, subtotal)\n    country = order.get(\"country\", \"US\")\n    tax = _tax_for(taxable, country)\n    shipping = _shipping_for(taxable, country)\n    total = round(taxable + tax + shipping, 2)\n    _add_customer_total(totals, order.get(\"customer\", \"unknown\"), total)\n    return total\n```\n\nThat's 18 lines. Good.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\ndef _add_customer_total(totals, customer, total):\n    entry = totals.setdefault(customer, {\"orders\": 0, \"spent\": 0.0})\n    entry[\"orders\"] += 1\n    entry[\"spent\"] = round(entry[\"spent\"] + total, 2)\n```\n\n```python\ndef _top_sku(sku_units):\n    if not sku_units:\n        return None\n    return sorted(sku_units.items(), key=lambda pair: (-pair[1], str(pair[0])))[0][0]\n\n\ndef _best_customer(totals):\n    best = None\n    for customer, entry in totals.items():\n        if best is None or entry[\"spent\"] > totals[best][\"spent\"]:\n            best = customer\n    return best\n```\n\nAnd the report:\n\n```python\ndef _build_report(totals, status_counts, sku_units, revenue, problems):\n    return {\n        \"revenue\": round(revenue, 2),\n        \"customers\": totals,\n        \"status_counts\": status_counts,\n        \"top_sku\": _top_sku(sku_units),\n        \"best_customer\": _best_customer(totals),\n        \"problems\": problems,\n    }\n```\n\nThe revenue accumulation: `revenue += total` where total is the rounded per-order total. In the main loop I need revenue accumulating across orders. If `_process_order` returns total (or None), then:\n\n```python\ndef summarize(orders):\n    totals = {}\n    status_counts = {}\n    sku_units = {}\n    problems = []\n    revenue = 0.0\n    for order in orders:\n        total = _process_order(order, totals, status_counts, sku_units, problems)\n        if total is not None:\n            revenue += total\n    return _build_report(...)\n```\n\nHmm, `total` could legitimately be 0.0 (e.g., order with no items and no tax → taxable 0, tax 0, shipping 4.99 → total 4.99; never 0.0 actually since shipping >= 4.99 unless taxable >= 50). Anyway `is not None` check is safe.\n\nBut `_process_order` has 5 params — a bit much but acceptable. Alternatively use a small dataclass-free accumulator class `_Report`/`_Accumulator`. Hmm, \"Use only the standard library\" — dataclasses is stdlib, fine. But the repo has no dependencies and is minimal; keeping it simple with plain functions is closer to the existing style.\n\nActually, let me consider a cleaner design: a `_Accumulator` class holding totals/status_counts/sku_units/problems/revenue with methods. But the instruction says \"Refactor it into small, well-named functions\". Functions are the requested unit. I'll go with functions but group the accumulators into a small container to avoid parameter soup... Actually 5 params is fine and